1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,240
Last video, I laid out the structure of a neural network.

2
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:10,880
I'll give a quick recap here, just so that it's fresh in our minds, and then I have

3
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:13,120
two main goals for this video.

4
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:17,640
The first is to introduce the idea of gradient descent, which underlies not only how neural

5
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:21,160
networks learn, but how a lot of other machine learning works as well.

6
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:24,080
Then after that, we're going to dig in a little more to how this particular network

7
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:29,520
performs, and what those hidden layers of neurons end up actually looking for.

8
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:34,640
As a reminder, our goal here is the classic example of handwritten digit recognition,

9
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:37,040
the hello world of neural networks.

10
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:42,120
These digits are rendered on a 28 by 28 pixel grid, each pixel with some grayscale value

11
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:44,240
between 0 and 1.

12
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:51,200
Those are what determine the activations of 784 neurons in the input layer of the network.

13
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:55,380
And then the activation for each neuron in the following layers is based on a weighted

14
00:00:55,380 --> 00:01:02,180
sum of all the activations in the previous layer, plus some special number called a bias.

15
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:06,460
Then you compose that sum with some other function, like the sigmoid squishification,

16
00:01:06,460 --> 00:01:09,620
or a reilu, the way that I walked through last video.

17
00:01:09,620 --> 00:01:14,060
In total, given the somewhat arbitrary choice of two hidden layers here with 16 neurons

18
00:01:14,060 --> 00:01:20,140
each, the network has about 13,000 weights and biases that we can adjust, and it's these

19
00:01:20,140 --> 00:01:25,260
values that determine what exactly the network actually does.

20
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:29,740
And what we mean when we say that this network classifies a given digit is that the brightest

21
00:01:29,740 --> 00:01:34,060
of those 10 neurons in the final layer corresponds to that digit.

22
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:38,180
And remember, the motivation that we had in mind here for the layered structure was that

23
00:01:38,180 --> 00:01:43,220
maybe the second layer could pick up on the edges, and the third layer might pick up on

24
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:47,660
patterns like loops and lines, and the last one could just piece together those patterns

25
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:49,780
to recognize digits.

26
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:52,860
So here we learn how the network learns.

27
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:56,900
What we want is an algorithm where you can show this network a whole bunch of training

28
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:01,540
data, which comes in the form of a bunch of different images of handwritten digits, along

29
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:06,380
with labels for what they're supposed to be, and it'll adjust those 13,000 weights

30
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:10,700
and biases so as to improve its performance on the training data.

31
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:15,540
Hopefully, this layered structure will mean that what it learns generalizes to images

32
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:17,820
beyond that training data.

33
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:21,780
And the way we test that is that after you train the network, you show it more labeled

34
00:02:21,780 --> 00:02:26,060
data that it's never seen before, and you see how accurately it classifies those new

35
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:31,060
images.

36
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:34,780
Fortunately for us, and what makes this such a common example to start with, is that the

37
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:39,500
good people behind the MNIST database have put together a collection of tens of thousands

38
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:43,660
of handwritten digit images, each one labeled with the numbers that they're supposed

39
00:02:43,660 --> 00:02:45,060
to be.

40
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:48,940
And as provocative as it is to describe a machine as learning, once you actually see

41
00:02:48,940 --> 00:02:54,140
how it works, it feels a lot less like some crazy sci-fi premise, and a lot more like

42
00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:55,460
a calculus exercise.

43
00:02:55,460 --> 00:03:01,020
I mean, basically it comes down to finding the minimum of a certain function.

44
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:06,460
Remember, conceptually, we're thinking of each neuron as being connected to all of the

45
00:03:06,460 --> 00:03:11,380
neurons in the previous layer, and the weights in the weighted sum defining its activation

46
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:14,220
are kind of like the strengths of those connections.

47
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:19,780
And the bias is some indication of whether that neuron tends to be active or inactive.

48
00:03:19,780 --> 00:03:23,300
And to start things off, we're just going to initialize all of those weights and biases

49
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:24,860
totally randomly.

50
00:03:24,860 --> 00:03:28,380
Needless to say, this network is going to perform pretty horribly on a given training

51
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:31,180
example, since it's just doing something random.

52
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:36,820
For example, you feed in this image of a 3, and the output layer just looks like a mess.

53
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:43,340
So what you do is you define a cost function, a way of telling the computer, no, bad computer.

54
00:03:43,340 --> 00:03:48,940
That output should have activations which are 0 for most neurons, but 1 for this neuron.

55
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:51,700
What you gave me is utter trash.

56
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:56,660
To say that a little more mathematically, what you do is add up the squares of the differences

57
00:03:56,660 --> 00:04:01,860
between each of those trash output activations and the value that you want them to have.

58
00:04:01,860 --> 00:04:05,500
And this is what we'll call the cost of a single training example.

59
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:12,620
Notice, this sum is small when the network confidently classifies the image correctly.

60
00:04:12,620 --> 00:04:18,780
But it's large when the network seems like it doesn't really know what it's doing.

61
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:23,840
So then what you do is consider the average cost over all of the tens of thousands of

62
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:27,540
training examples at your disposal.

63
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:32,260
This average cost is our measure for how lousy the network is, and how bad the computer should

64
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:33,260
feel.

65
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:35,260
And that's a complicated thing.

66
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:38,260
Remember how the network itself was basically a function?

67
00:04:38,260 --> 00:04:44,140
One that takes in 784 numbers as inputs, the pixel values, and spits out 10 numbers as

68
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:45,420
its output.

69
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:49,620
And in a sense, it's parameterized by all these weights and biases.

70
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:53,300
Well the cost function is a layer of complexity on top of that.

71
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:59,700
It takes as its input those 13,000 or so weights and biases, and it spits out a single number,

72
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:02,700
describing how bad those weights and biases are.

73
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:07,700
And the way it's defined depends on the network's behavior over all the tens of thousands of

74
00:05:07,700 --> 00:05:09,100
pieces of training data.

75
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:12,460
That's a lot to think about.

76
00:05:12,460 --> 00:05:16,380
But just telling the computer what a crappy job it's doing isn't very helpful.

77
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:21,300
You want to tell it how to change those weights and biases so that it gets better.

78
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:25,780
To make it easier, rather than struggling to imagine a function with 13,000 inputs, just

79
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:31,420
imagine a simple function that has one number as an input and one number as an output.

80
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:36,740
How do you find an input that minimizes the value of this function?

81
00:05:37,740 --> 00:05:42,300
You can sometimes figure out that minimum explicitly, but that's not always feasible

82
00:05:42,300 --> 00:05:47,900
for really complicated functions, certainly not in the 13,000 input version of this situation

83
00:05:47,900 --> 00:05:51,660
for our crazy complicated neural network cost function.

84
00:05:51,660 --> 00:05:56,820
A more flexible tactic is to start at any old input and figure out which direction you

85
00:05:56,820 --> 00:05:59,980
should step to make that output lower.

86
00:05:59,980 --> 00:06:05,020
Specifically, if you can figure out the slope of the function where you are, then shift

87
00:06:05,020 --> 00:06:09,300
to the left if that slope is positive, and shift the input to the right if that slope

88
00:06:09,300 --> 00:06:12,740
is negative.

89
00:06:12,740 --> 00:06:16,700
If you do this repeatedly, at each point checking the new slope and taking the appropriate

90
00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:20,700
step, you're going to approach some local minimum of the function.

91
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:24,620
And the image you might have in mind here is a ball rolling down a hill.

92
00:06:24,620 --> 00:06:29,380
And notice, even for this really simplified single input function, there are many possible

93
00:06:29,380 --> 00:06:34,100
valleys that you might land in, depending on which random input you start at, and there's

94
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:38,460
no guarantee that the local minimum you land in is going to be the smallest possible value

95
00:06:38,460 --> 00:06:40,180
of the cost function.

96
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:43,180
That's going to carry over to our neural network case as well.

97
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:48,180
And I also want you to notice how if you make your step sizes proportional to the slope,

98
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:52,900
then when the slope is flattening out towards the minimum, your steps get smaller and smaller,

99
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:56,140
and that kind of helps you from overshooting.

100
00:06:56,140 --> 00:07:01,620
Bumping up the complexity a bit, imagine instead a function with two inputs and one output.

101
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:06,340
You might think of the input space as the xy plane and the cost function as being graphed

102
00:07:06,340 --> 00:07:08,740
as a surface above it.

103
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:13,180
Now instead of asking about the slope of the function, you have to ask which direction

104
00:07:13,180 --> 00:07:18,580
should you step in this input space so as to decrease the output of the function most

105
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:19,780
quickly.

106
00:07:19,780 --> 00:07:22,340
In other words, what's the downhill direction?

107
00:07:22,340 --> 00:07:26,740
And again, it's helpful to think of a ball rolling down that hill.

108
00:07:26,740 --> 00:07:31,940
Those of you familiar with multivariable calculus will know that the gradient of a function

109
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:36,740
gives you the direction of steepest ascent, basically which direction should you step

110
00:07:36,740 --> 00:07:39,500
to increase the function most quickly.

111
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:43,820
Naturally enough, taking the negative of that gradient gives you the direction to step that

112
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:47,300
decreases the function most quickly.

113
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:51,100
And even more than that, the length of this gradient vector is actually an indication

114
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:54,580
for just how steep that steepest slope is.

115
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:57,940
Now if you're unfamiliar with multivariable calculus and you want to learn more, check

116
00:07:57,940 --> 00:08:01,020
out some of the work that I did for Khan Academy on the topic.

117
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:05,700
Honestly though, all that matters for you and me right now is that in principle there

118
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:10,460
exists a way to compute this vector, this vector that tells you what the downhill direction

119
00:08:10,460 --> 00:08:12,060
is and how steep it is.

120
00:08:12,060 --> 00:08:17,260
You'll be okay if that's all you know and you're not rock solid on the details.

121
00:08:17,260 --> 00:08:21,460
Because if you can get that, the algorithm for minimizing the function is to compute

122
00:08:21,460 --> 00:08:27,660
this gradient direction, then take a small step downhill and just repeat that over and

123
00:08:27,660 --> 00:08:28,660
over.

124
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:33,700
It's the same basic idea for a function that has 13,000 inputs instead of two inputs.

125
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:38,980
Imagine organizing all 13,000 weights and biases of our network into a giant column

126
00:08:38,980 --> 00:08:40,140
vector.

127
00:08:40,140 --> 00:08:44,420
The negative gradient of the cost function is just a vector.

128
00:08:44,420 --> 00:08:50,140
It's some direction inside this insanely huge input space that tells you which nudges

129
00:08:50,140 --> 00:08:55,860
to all of those numbers is going to cause the most rapid decrease to the cost function.

130
00:08:55,860 --> 00:08:59,980
And of course, with our specially designed cost function, changing the weights and biases

131
00:08:59,980 --> 00:09:05,500
to decrease it means making the output of the network on each piece of training data

132
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:10,180
look less like a random array of 10 values and more like an actual decision that we want

133
00:09:10,180 --> 00:09:11,180
it to make.

134
00:09:11,180 --> 00:09:15,940
It's important to remember, this cost function involves an average over all of the training

135
00:09:15,940 --> 00:09:16,940
data.

136
00:09:16,940 --> 00:09:24,260
If you minimize it, it means it's a better performance on all of those samples.

137
00:09:24,260 --> 00:09:28,540
The algorithm for computing this gradient efficiently, which is effectively the heart

138
00:09:28,540 --> 00:09:32,060
of how a neural network learns, is called back propagation.

